        title: "Self-introduction with 我是 (wǒ shì)",
        explanation:
          "To introduce yourself, use the pattern: 我是 + [Name]. 是 (shì) means \"to be\" and works like \"am/is/are\" in English.",
        examples: [
          { target: "我是王明。", english: "I am Wang Ming." },
          { target: "我是學生。", english: "I am a student." },
        ],
      },
      {
        title: "Asking questions with 嗎 (ma)",
        explanation:
          "To turn a statement into a yes/no question, simply add 嗎 (ma) at the end. No word order change needed.",
        examples: [
          { target: "你好嗎？", english: "How are you?" },
          { target: "你是老師嗎？", english: "Are you a teacher?" },
        ],
      },
    ],
    quizId: "zh-quiz-001",
  },
  {
    id: "zh-lesson-002",
    languageCode: "zh",
    title: "Basic Verbs & Actions",
    description: "Learn common everyday verbs and how to use them in sentences.",
    order: 2,
    vocabularyIds: [
      "zh-vocab-008",
      "zh-vocab-009",
      "zh-vocab-010",
      "zh-vocab-011",
    ],
    grammar: [
      {
        title: "Subject-Verb-Object word order",
        explanation:
          "Chinese follows SVO (Subject-Verb-Object) order, similar to English. Unlike many Asian languages, Chinese verbs do not conjugate — the same form is used regardless of tense or person.",
        examples: [
          { target: "我吃早餐。", english: "I eat breakfast." },
          { target: "他看電影。", english: "He watches a movie." },
        ],
      },
      {
        title: "Negation with 不 (bù)",
        explanation:
          "Place 不 (bù) before a verb to negate it. For the verb 有 (to have), use 沒 (méi) instead.",
        examples: [
          { target: "我不喝咖啡。", english: "I don't drink coffee." },
          { target: "他不去學校。", english: "He doesn't go to school." },
        ],
      },
    ],
    quizId: "zh-quiz-002",
  },
  {
    id: "zh-lesson-003",
    languageCode: "zh",
    title: "Everyday Nouns & Adjectives",
    description: "Build your vocabulary with common nouns and descriptive adjectives.",
    order: 3,
    vocabularyIds: [
      "zh-vocab-012",
      "zh-vocab-013",
      "zh-vocab-014",
      "zh-vocab-015",
      "zh-vocab-016",
      "zh-vocab-017",
      "zh-vocab-018",
      "zh-vocab-019",
      "zh-vocab-020",
    ],
    grammar: [
      {
        title: "Adjectives with 很 (hěn)",
        explanation:
          "In Chinese, adjectives act as verbs and don't need 是. Use 很 (hěn, \"very\") before adjectives in simple descriptive sentences. Without 很, the sentence implies a comparison.",
        examples: [
          { target: "貓很小。", english: "The cat is small." },
          { target: "這本書很新。", english: "This book is new." },
        ],
      },
      {
        title: "Measure words with 一 + measure word + noun",
        explanation:
          "Chinese uses measure words (classifiers) between a number and a noun. The most common general measure word is 個 (gè). 本 (běn) is used for books, 隻 (zhī) for animals.",
        examples: [
          { target: "一個朋友", english: "a friend" },
          { target: "一本書", english: "a book" },
        ],
      },
    ],
  },
];

